# Cloudflare Pages security headers (KTD9, KTD12).
#
# Source of truth for the CSP directives is src/trust/csp.ts; this file is
# hand-kept in sync with it and checked for drift by src/trust/csp.test.ts.
#
# https://huggingface.co, https://*.hf.co (the Xet weight CDN — regional hosts
# like us.aws.cdn.hf.co / eu.aws.cdn.hf.co, see src/trust/csp.ts) and the older
# https://cdn-lfs*.huggingface.co hosts in connect-src are the ONLY allowed
# third-party origins in this entire policy, and only for the one-time model
# downloads (Whisper onnx-community/whisper-large-v3-turbo + the Phase D
# diarization set). No other outgoing connection is technically possible
# (R10) — "nichts verlässt das Gerät".
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy / Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy (KTD4, revised).
# The original KTD4 shipped WITHOUT these: WebGPU doesn't need cross-origin
# isolation, and `require-corp` would have tied the Hugging Face model
# download's redirect chain (huggingface.co -> cdn-lfs*/Xet regional hosts)
# to header discipline on servers we don't control. (Strictly: CORP gating
# hits no-cors subresources, and the model downloads are CORS fetches — it
# would probably have worked. "Probably", on the app's one critical network
# operation, was the unacceptable part.) That
# was the right call at the time, and the price was paid in full on real
# hardware: the diarization worker's ONNX Runtime WASM binary
# (ort-wasm-simd-threaded.asyncify) can only ever see a `SharedArrayBuffer`
# under cross-origin isolation, so without COOP/COEP it silently falls back
# to ONE thread — a 58-minute recording took ~30 min to transcribe (WebGPU,
# fine) but 58+ min just to annotate speakers (pyannote segmentation +
# WeSpeaker embeddings, both WASM), at ~13 % CPU on a 14-core machine.
#
# `COEP: credentialless` (vs. `require-corp`) is the fix that keeps both
# constraints satisfied: it isolates the browsing context (SharedArrayBuffer
# becomes available) WITHOUT requiring a CORP header from cross-origin
# responses — those responses are instead loaded as if fetched with no
# credentials, which is exactly what the HF model download already does. No
# CORS/CORP change on the Hugging Face side is needed, so the download is not
# put at risk. Support: Chromium and Firefox (119+) understand
# `credentialless`; Safari does not (as of writing). Acceptable — the fast
# path already requires WebGPU anyway. Browsers that
# don't understand `credentialless` simply don't isolate — no SharedArrayBuffer,
# ORT WASM stays single-threaded, same graceful behaviour as before this
# change (SD-3) — they must NOT be broken by these headers, only left
# un-isolated. See `src/worker/model/ortWasmPaths.ts`'s `resolveOrtNumThreads`
# for the thread-count decision this isolation unlocks.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://huggingface.co https://*.hf.co https://cdn-lfs.huggingface.co https://cdn-lfs-us-1.huggingface.co https://cdn-lfs-eu-1.huggingface.co; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), microphone=(self)
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
